  "hints": [
    "Millions of phones sending updates every few seconds need persistent connections. Which component holds them?",
    "Assigning a driver is check-then-write: \"is this driver free?\" followed by \"assign\". What stops two requests from doing that at once?",
    "WebSocket Gateway (3) \u2192 Dispatch (15) \u2192 Cache (2) + NoSQL (3), and Dispatch \u2192 ZooKeeper (3 nodes) for driver locks."
  ],
  "scoring": {
    "weights": {
      "performance": 0.35,
      "reliability": 0.3,
      "cost": 0.15,
      "architecture": 0.2
    },
    "latencyTargetMs": 50,
    "costBudget": 2400
  },
  "explanation": [
    "Location updates are a firehose of small writes. A partitioned NoSQL store spreads them across shards, and a cache serves \"drivers near me\" lookups.",
    "Driver assignment is a distributed race. A lock (ZooKeeper or Redis) per driver makes the check-then-assign atomic across all dispatch servers.",
    "ZooKeeper needs a quorum, so run 3 nodes: one can fail without losing locking.",
    "Real systems index locations geospatially (geohash or H3 cells) so \"nearby\" becomes a key lookup instead of a distance calculation over every driver."
  ]
}
